<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Page Configurations</h1>
<p class="block-content">
Page configurations are mainly used by <tt>TPageService</tt> to modify or append the application configuration. As the name indicates, a page configuration is associated with a directory storing some page files. It is stored as a file named <tt>config.xml</tt>, or <tt>config.php</tt> when the application uses the PHP form.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
When a user requests a page stored under <tt>&lt;BasePath&gt;/dir1/dir2</tt>, the <tt>TPageService</tt> will try to parse and load <tt>config.xml</tt> files under <tt>&lt;BasePath&gt;</tt>, <tt>&lt;BasePath&gt;/dir1</tt> and <tt>&lt;BasePath&gt;/dir1/dir2</tt>. Paths, modules, and parameters specified in these configuration files will be appended or merged into the existing application configuration. Here <tt>&lt;BasePath&gt;</tt> is as defined in <a href="?page=Fundamentals.Services">page service</a>.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
The format of a page configuration file is as follows,
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<configuration>
  <paths>
    <alias id="AliasID" path="AliasPath" />
    <using namespace="Namespace" />
  </paths>
  <modules>
    <module id="ModuleID" class="ModuleClass"  PropertyName="PropertyValue" ... />
  </modules>
  <parameters>
    <parameter id="ParameterID" class="ParameterClass" PropertyName="PropertyValue" ... />
  </parameters>
  <include file="path.to.extconfig" when="PHP expression" />
  <authorization>
    <allow pages="PageID1,PageID2" users="User1,User2" roles="Role1,Role2" verb="get" />
    <deny pages="PageID1,PageID2" users="User1,User2" roles="Role1,Role2" verb="post" />
  </authorization>
  <pages PropertyName="PropertyValue" ...>
    <page id="PageID" PropertyName="PropertyValue" ... />
  </pages>
</configuration>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
<?php
return [
  'paths' => [
    'aliases' => ['AliasID' => 'AliasPath'],
    'using' => ['Namespace'],
  ],
  'modules' => [
    'ModuleID' => [
      'class' => 'ModuleClass',
      'properties' => ['PropertyName' => 'PropertyValue'],
    ],
  ],
  'parameters' => [
    'ParameterID' => [
      'class' => 'ParameterClass',
      'properties' => ['PropertyName' => 'PropertyValue'],
    ],
  ],
  'includes' => [
    ['file' => 'path.to.extconfig', 'when' => 'PHP expression'],
  ],
  'authorization' => [
    ['action' => 'allow', 'pages' => 'PageID1,PageID2',
     'users' => 'User1,User2', 'roles' => 'Role1,Role2', 'verb' => 'get'],
    ['action' => 'deny', 'pages' => 'PageID1,PageID2',
     'users' => 'User1,User2', 'roles' => 'Role1,Role2', 'verb' => 'post'],
  ],
  'pages' => [
    'properties' => ['PropertyName' => 'PropertyValue'],
    'PageID' => [
      'properties' => ['PropertyName' => 'PropertyValue'],
    ],
  ],
];
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>&lt;paths&gt;</tt>, <tt>&lt;modules&gt;</tt>, <tt>&lt;parameters&gt;</tt> and <tt>&lt;include&gt;</tt> are similar to those in an application configuration. The <tt>&lt;authorization&gt;</tt> element specifies the authorization rules that apply to the current page directory and all its subdirectories. For more details, see <a href="?page=Advanced.Auth">authentication and authorization</a> section. The <tt>&lt;pages&gt;</tt> element specifies the initial values for the properties of pages. Each <tt>&lt;page&gt;</tt> element specifies the initial property values for a particular page identified by the <tt>id</tt> attribute. Initial property values given in the <tt>&lt;pages&gt;</tt> element apply to all pages in the current directory and all its subdirectories.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Complete specification of page configurations can be found in the <a href="https://github.com/pradosoft/prado/blob/master/docs/specs/config.dtd">DTD</a> and <a href="https://github.com/pradosoft/prado/blob/master/docs/specs/config.xsd">XSD</a> files.
</p>

<h2>Page paths and wildcards</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1.1" />
<p class="block-content">
The <tt>id</tt> attribute of a <tt>&lt;page&gt;</tt> element can be a relative page path rather than a bare page name, so one file configures pages in the directories below it. <tt>id="admin.Home"</tt> names the <tt>Home</tt> page under <tt>admin</tt>, and <tt>id="admin.*"</tt> names every page under <tt>admin</tt> and its subdirectories. This is how an application keeps its page initialization in one place, in the application configuration or in the configuration at the root of the page tree, rather than scattering a file through every directory.
</p>

<h2>The PHP form</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.2" />
<p class="block-content">
A page configuration follows the format the application chose. An application constructed for PHP configuration reads <tt>config.php</tt> in each page directory instead of <tt>config.xml</tt>, holding the same information as a PHP array. The choice is made once, in the entry script, and is described under <a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">application configurations</a>.
</p>
<p class="block-content">
Two elements change shape. The <tt>&lt;allow&gt;</tt> and <tt>&lt;deny&gt;</tt> elements
become entries of one <tt>authorization</tt> list, each carrying an <tt>action</tt> key that
says which of the two it is. Each <tt>&lt;page&gt;</tt> element becomes an entry of the
<tt>pages</tt> array keyed by the page path, and the properties that the
<tt>&lt;pages&gt;</tt> element itself carries go under the reserved key
<tt>properties</tt>.
</p>

<h2>Extending how a page configuration loads</h2>
<com:SinceVersion Version="4.2.0" />
<p class="block-content">
<tt>TPageConfiguration</tt> raises three dynamic events while it works, so a
<a href="?page=Fundamentals.Behaviors">behavior</a> attached to it can take part in
loading without the class changing.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Dynamic event</th><th>Raised</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>dyLoadPageConfigurationFromXml</tt></td>
	<td>After an XML configuration file is read, with the parsed document.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>dyLoadPageConfigurationFromPhp</tt></td>
	<td>The same, for a PHP configuration file, with the array.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>dyApplyConfiguration</tt></td>
	<td>When the collected configuration is applied to the page service.</td>
</tr>
</table>

</com:TContent>
